// std.ports.mechanical CI signal — examples/stdlib/ports_mechanical.ri
//
// Demonstrates the std.ports.mechanical trait surface via a generic shaft
// coupling that accepts any two RotaryPort-typed connections.
//
// This file is auto-covered by examples_smoke.rs (directory walk) and
// additionally compiled by the capstone test in ports_stdlib_compile.rs.

import std.ports.mechanical

// A generic shaft coupling that connects a driver port to a driven port.
// D and N are type parameters constrained to RotaryPort; no concrete
// RotaryPort conformer is instantiated here, which keeps this zero-Error
// signal free of avoidable risk (PRD §4 decision 4). A conformer would need
// frame, max_speed, max_torque, and axis literals; the ground truth for that
// set is rotary_port_concrete_conformer_compiles in ports_stdlib_compile.rs.
//
// Named ShaftCoupling (not Coupling) to avoid colliding with the unrelated
// stdlib `Coupling<P: DrivingJoint + HasMotion>` kinematic joint type
// declared in crates/reify-compiler/stdlib/kinematic.ri — same name,
// different semantics, different type params/bounds, and an unqualified
// `Coupling` here would silently shadow the stdlib one in this module's
// template list (task #5594).
structure def ShaftCoupling<D: RotaryPort, N: RotaryPort> {
    port driver : D
    port driven : N
}
